Package: cfisher
Type: Package
Title: Fisher's Exact Test Controlling for Confounders
Version: 0.1.0
Date: 2026-11-05
Authors@R: c(
    person("Zheyang", "Wu", email = "zheyangwu@wpi.edu", role = c("aut", "cre", "cph")),
    person("Gonzalo", "Contador", email = "gonzalo.contador@usm.cl", role = "aut")
    )
Description: Tests the association between a binary exposure and a binary outcome while
    controlling for confounders, in the manner of Fisher's exact test. A logistic model of
    the outcome on the confounders, without the exposure, gives each subject's probability
    of being a case under the null hypothesis. The test then uses the exact null
    distribution of the number of exposed cases, either given the exposed subjects and the
    number of cases (a generalization of the hypergeometric distribution of Fisher's exact
    test) or given the exposed subjects only (a Poisson-binomial distribution). It returns
    the p-value, an estimate of the odds ratio and its confidence interval. With
    confounders the fitted model is plugged in, so the p-value and the interval are exact
    at the true confounder effects. Without confounders the results agree with
    'fisher.test()'. The null model can be fitted once and reused, which suits scans of
    many exposures, such as genetic variants, against one outcome. The method is described
    in a draft manuscript by Wu and Contador (2026), Fisher's exact test controlling for
    confounders.
